<article class="env-lemma" id="0CE7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CE7">Lemma <span data-tag="0CE7">55.4.3</span>.</a> Let $n, m_ i, a_{ij}, w_ i, g_ i$ be a numerical type $T$. Then $\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (T) \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(A)$ where $A = (a_{ij})$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (T)$ is the cokernel of $(a_{ij}/w_ i)$ we see that there is a commutative diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ 0 \ar[r] &  \mathbf{Z}^{\oplus n} \ar[rr]_ A &  &  \mathbf{Z}^{\oplus n} \ar[rr] &  &  \mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(A) \ar[r] &  0 \\  0 \ar[r] &  \mathbf{Z}^{\oplus n} \ar[rr]^{(a_{ij}/w_ i)} \ar[u]_{\text{id}} &  &  \mathbf{Z}^{\oplus n} \ar[rr] \ar[u]_{\text{diag}(w_1, \ldots , w_ n)} &  &  \mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (T) \ar[r] \ar[u] &  0 }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       with exact rows. By the snake lemma we conclude that $\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (T) \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(A)$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>